  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor satellite and upper air analysis \nshow a sharp deep layer trough quickly progressing eastward over the \nUpper Midwest, and a broad mid/upper ridge out over the southwestern \nCONUS. At the surface, an area of low pressure is moving eastward \nacross Iowa with a frontal boundary draped across northern Missouri \nand central Kansas. Out ahead of the front, light winds and clear \nskies are contributing to optimal radiational cooling, and there's \nsufficient low-level moisture for areas of dense fog to develop \nacross portions of south-central and southeast Kansas. As the \naforementioned frontal boundary pushes southeast this morning, \nincreasing northwesterly winds will usher in a drier air mass, and \nfog should dissipate from northwest to southeast through the morning \nhours. Still, portions of far southeast Kansas could see dense fog \nlinger through 15-16Z this morning before the front passes through. \nA fairly strong surface ridge sinking southward into the\nnorthern/central CONUS will contribute to a tight pressure \ngradient across the area today. As a result, sustained northwest\nwinds will be around 15 to 25 mph with gusts around 30 to 35 \nmph this afternoon. Surface winds will be lighter on Thursday, \nbut will remain northerly which will continue the advection a \ncooler airmass into the forecast area throughout the day. As a \nresult, afternoon highs will only rise into the 50s and low 60s.\nLight winds and clear skies will allow for ample radiational \ncooling Thursday night and Friday morning supporting \ntemperatures near or slightly below freezing area wide. Friday \nwill be a little cooler with afternoon highs in the low to mid \n50s, and nighttime lows around freezing.\n\nMid range global guidance continues to remain consistent with \nshowing a deep layer trough skating across the central CONUS this \nweekend. As such, rain chances continue to increase for late \nSaturday through early Monday. Generally speaking, this will be \nsupported by broad synoptic ascent overspreading the central plains. \nHowever, little to no instability will limit any thunderstorm \npotential. Additionally, another push of cooler air will send \nafternoon temperatures down into the 40s on Sunday. Heading into \nnext week, the return of southerly low-level flow will introduce a \nslight warming trend. Long range guidance beyond the forecast period \ncontinues to show increased probabilities for above average \nprecipitation late next week into the following weekend, and details \nshould come into better focus for this period over the coming\ndays.",
